\description{
Inserts a chat UI element into a Shiny UI, which includes a scrollable
section for displaying chat messages, and an input field for the user to
enter new messages.

To respond to user input, listen for \code{input$ID_user_input} (for example, if
\code{id="my_chat"}, user input will be at \code{input$my_chat_user_input}), and use
\code{\link[=chat_append]{chat_append()}} to append messages to the chat.

The chat also reports the client's current rendered message transcript as
\code{input$ID_messages} (for example, \code{input$my_chat_messages}), tagged
\code{shinychat.messages}. It updates every time a message finishes rendering
or streaming (a "settle point"), and is a list of message objects:
\code{list(role =, segments = list(list(content =, content_type =), ...))},
plus optional \code{htmlDeps} and \code{attachments} fields when present.
\code{\link[=chat_enable_history]{chat_enable_history()}} reads this internally to persist and restore
exactly what was rendered — including raw HTML and Shiny UI dependencies —
across a conversation switch or reload. It's exposed for advanced,
read-only use (for example, custom logging or export); it is not an input
you write to.
}
\section{Pairing with \code{chat_server()}}{


\code{chat_ui(id)} and \code{chat_server(id, client)} pair by matching \code{id}. This
works the same way at the top level of an app and inside your own Shiny
module — \code{chat_server()} is not itself a module, so no \code{NS(id, "chat")}
wrapping is required:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# Top-level app, no module
ui <- page_fillable(chat_ui("chat"))
server <- function(input, output, session) \{
  chat_server("chat", client)
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# Inside your own module: pass the same literal id to both, and call
# chat_server() from inside moduleServer() so it inherits the module's
# already-namespaced `session`
mod_ui <- function(id) \{
  ns <- NS(id)
  chat_ui(ns("chat"))
\}
mod_server <- function(id, client) \{
  moduleServer(id, function(input, output, session) \{
    chat_server("chat", client)
  \})
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Greeting}{


A greeting is an optional welcome message shown before any conversation
messages. It is automatically dismissed when the user sends their first
message (unless created with \code{persistent = TRUE}).

\strong{Static greeting.} Pass a string or \code{\link[=chat_greeting]{chat_greeting()}} to the \code{greeting}
parameter:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{chat_ui("chat", greeting = "## Hello!\\n\\nHow can I help you today?")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Dynamic greeting from the server.} Leave \code{greeting} unset and use
\code{\link[=chat_set_greeting]{chat_set_greeting()}} from your server function. This is useful when the
greeting depends on session state or is generated by a model.

\strong{\code{greeting_requested} input.} When the chat is visible on the page, has
no messages, and has no greeting set, Shiny fires
\verb{input$<id>_greeting_requested} (e.g. \code{input$chat_greeting_requested} for
\code{chat_ui("chat")}). The value is an event counter suitable for
\code{\link[shiny:observeEvent]{shiny::observeEvent()}}. Use it to trigger server-side greeting generation:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{observeEvent(input$chat_greeting_requested, \{
  stream <- chat_client$stream_async("Generate a short welcome message.")
  chat_set_greeting("chat", chat_greeting(stream))
\})
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

This input fires when the chat component is first viewed on the page and
empty, and again after \code{\link[=chat_clear]{chat_clear()}} \code{(greeting = TRUE)}, enabling a
regenerate pattern where clearing the greeting automatically triggers a
fresh one.

\strong{\code{greeting_dismissed} input.} When the user dismisses the greeting,
\verb{input$<id>_greeting_dismissed} fires with a \code{Date.now()} timestamp. If the
greeting is later cleared after being dismissed, the input resets to \code{NULL}.
If you use \code{\link[=chat_server]{chat_server()}}, you can access the \code{greeting_dismissed}
reactive from the returned value instead of the raw namespaced input
string.
}

\section{Thinking display}{


When a model produces reasoning or "thinking" tokens, shinychat renders them
in a collapsible panel above the response. The panel shows a live stream of
the model's reasoning while it thinks, then auto-collapses when the response
begins.

Thinking display works automatically with any model that supports it. Two
paths are supported:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{ellmer's \code{ContentThinking} objects.} Models that provide a structured
thinking API (e.g., Claude with extended thinking) emit \code{ContentThinking}
objects when you stream with \code{stream = "content"}. shinychat detects these
and routes them to the thinking panel. This is what \code{chat_append()} uses
internally when you pass it an ellmer content stream.
\item \strong{Raw \verb{<thinking>} tags.} Many open-source and local models (DeepSeek,
QwQ, Qwen, etc.) emit \verb{<thinking>...</thinking>} tags directly in their
markdown output. shinychat detects these tags during streaming and renders
the enclosed text in the thinking panel with no extra configuration.
}
\subsection{Topic labels}{

You can optionally get labeled sub-sections within the thinking panel by
asking the model to emit \verb{<topic>...</topic>} tags in its reasoning. These
are extracted and rendered as section headings inside the thinking display,
and the current topic appears in the collapsed header as a live status.

To use topic labels, add something like this to your system prompt:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{When thinking through a problem, wrap brief topic labels in <topic> tags
to indicate what you're currently reasoning about. For example:
<topic>parsing the input</topic>
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Topic labels are entirely optional. Without them, the thinking panel still
works -- it just won't have sub-section headings.
}
}

\section{Customizing the send button}{


The send button is a filled circle (24px by default) whose background
color reflects the current state (primary when ready, gray when
empty/disabled, danger when cancelling) with a white icon (22px by
default) centered inside. The \code{icon_send} parameter swaps the
ready-state icon without changing the button's surface.

\strong{Custom icon.} Pass an SVG from \code{\link[bsicons:bs_icon]{bsicons::bs_icon()}} or
\code{\link[faicons:icon_svg]{faicons::icon_svg()}}. The button provides the surface, so a bare glyph
gets the same filled-circle treatment as the default arrow:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{chat_ui("chat", icon_send = bsicons::bs_icon("send-fill"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Icon with text.} Use \code{\link[htmltools:tagList]{htmltools::tagList()}} to pass an icon and a text
label as siblings (not wrapped in a \verb{<span>}) so they lay out side by
side, with a Bootstrap margin utility for spacing. Then override the
button to size to its content instead of the default fixed circle:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{chat_ui("chat",
  icon_send = tagList(
    bsicons::bs_icon("airplane-fill"),
    span("Send", class = "ms-2")
  )
)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode css">}}\preformatted{:root .shiny-chat-btn-send \{
  width: auto;
  height: auto;
  padding: 4px 10px;
  border-radius: 6px;
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Per-state color overrides.} Each state's color can be set independently
via CSS variables on the chat container or any ancestor. These are only
read by the component (never set on the button), so inline styles inherit
cleanly:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode css">}}\preformatted{#chat \{
  --shiny-chat-btn-send-color-cancel: #abc123;
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Ghost (outline) style.} Make the button transparent at rest with the
state color on the icon and border, filling on hover. Target the button
element (not an ancestor) because the internal \verb{--_btn-send-state-color}
variable resolves on the button itself:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode css">}}\preformatted{:root .shiny-chat-btn-send \{
  --shiny-chat-btn-send-bg: transparent;
  --shiny-chat-btn-send-color: var(--_btn-send-state-color);
  --shiny-chat-btn-send-border: 1px solid var(--_btn-send-state-color);
  --shiny-chat-btn-send-color-hover: #fff;
  --shiny-chat-btn-send-bg-hover: var(--_btn-send-state-color);
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\strong{Key CSS variables:}
\itemize{
\item \code{--shiny-chat-btn-send-size} — Button width and height (default \verb{24px})
\item \code{--shiny-chat-input-icon-size} — Icon size, shared with the attach button (default \verb{22px})
\item \code{--shiny-chat-btn-send-bg} — Button background (default: state color)
\item \code{--shiny-chat-btn-send-color} — Icon color (default: \verb{#fff})
\item \code{--shiny-chat-btn-send-border} — Button border (default: \code{none})
\item \code{--shiny-chat-btn-send-color-ready} — Override ready/pending color (default: \code{--bs-primary})
\item \code{--shiny-chat-btn-send-color-empty} — Override empty/disabled color (default: \code{--bs-gray-500})
\item \code{--shiny-chat-btn-send-color-cancel} — Override cancel/cancelling color (default: \code{--bs-danger})
}
}

